WEBVTT

00:00:04.500 --> 00:00:06.440
An agent can change twenty files in

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:07.480
thirty seconds.

00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:09.180
Bundle them into one commit,

00:00:09.180 --> 00:00:10.550
and when something breaks,

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.080
you're untangling a mess.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.300
Split them into five clean commits,

00:00:14.300 --> 00:00:16.060
and you revert the one that broke

00:00:16.060 --> 00:00:17.540
and keep the rest.

00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:18.580
With agents,

00:00:18.580 --> 00:00:20.720
Git stops being just version control.

00:00:20.720 --> 00:00:22.340
It becomes the backbone:

00:00:22.340 --> 00:00:23.570
your undo button,

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:24.830
your parallel workspace,

00:00:25.080 --> 00:00:26.160
your review screen,

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:27.510
and your documentation.

00:00:28.542 --> 00:00:29.482
The first habit:

00:00:29.482 --> 00:00:30.102
commit small,

00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:31.302
and commit often.

00:00:31.302 --> 00:00:33.362
Commit after each logical change,

00:00:33.362 --> 00:00:34.742
after the tests pass,

00:00:34.742 --> 00:00:36.042
and before anything risky,

00:00:36.042 --> 00:00:37.802
so there's always a save point to

00:00:37.802 --> 00:00:38.652
return to.

00:00:39.323 --> 00:00:40.783
Then let the agent write the commit

00:00:40.783 --> 00:00:41.443
message.

00:00:41.443 --> 00:00:43.303
It has the whole diff in context,

00:00:43.303 --> 00:00:45.623
so it summarizes from facts,

00:00:45.623 --> 00:00:46.573
not memory.

00:00:46.903 --> 00:00:48.913
Fix auth bug is noise.

00:00:48.943 --> 00:00:51.223
A message that explains the race condition

00:00:51.223 --> 00:00:53.683
and why it happened is useful six

00:00:53.683 --> 00:00:54.523
months later.

00:00:54.523 --> 00:00:56.023
Add a co -author line,

00:00:56.023 --> 00:00:57.863
so agent work is searchable.

00:00:57.863 --> 00:00:59.573
Your name is still on the commit.

00:01:00.292 --> 00:01:02.572
Every agent task gets its own branch.

00:01:02.572 --> 00:01:04.102
That gives you isolation,

00:01:04.152 --> 00:01:05.492
a clear review scope,

00:01:05.492 --> 00:01:06.882
and a clean rollback:

00:01:06.892 --> 00:01:08.132
if it's wrong,

00:01:08.132 --> 00:01:09.232
delete the branch.

00:01:09.232 --> 00:01:11.212
Name branches after the task,

00:01:11.212 --> 00:01:13.252
and your branch list becomes a manifest

00:01:13.252 --> 00:01:14.822
of what your agents are doing.

00:01:15.630 --> 00:01:17.240
For agents working in parallel,

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.510
branches aren't enough.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.470
Two agents in one folder will fight

00:01:20.470 --> 00:01:21.500
over the files.

00:01:21.510 --> 00:01:23.430
A work tree gives each agent its

00:01:23.430 --> 00:01:25.410
own directory on its own branch,

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:26.680
sharing one history.

00:01:26.690 --> 00:01:28.830
It's the cheapest sandbox there is.

00:01:28.830 --> 00:01:30.590
Just remember it holds only what Git

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:31.250
tracks,

00:01:31.250 --> 00:01:33.830
so no environment files and no install

00:01:33.830 --> 00:01:34.680
dependencies.

00:01:35.551 --> 00:01:37.331
More and more tools create work trees

00:01:37.331 --> 00:01:38.171
for you,

00:01:38.171 --> 00:01:39.991
and cloud agents clone the repo into

00:01:39.991 --> 00:01:42.031
a remote sandbox and hand back a

00:01:42.031 --> 00:01:42.811
pull request.

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:45.051
The shape is the same every time.

00:01:45.051 --> 00:01:46.511
An isolated copy,

00:01:46.511 --> 00:01:47.871
a dedicated branch,

00:01:47.871 --> 00:01:49.281
and a diff you review.

00:01:50.089 --> 00:01:52.349
Your review interface is the diff.

00:01:52.349 --> 00:01:53.839
Read the test first,

00:01:53.849 --> 00:01:55.299
watch for scope creep,

00:01:55.349 --> 00:01:56.699
and check the interfaces.

00:01:56.829 --> 00:01:58.639
When agents run in parallel,

00:01:58.709 --> 00:02:00.669
reviewing becomes the bottleneck.

00:02:00.669 --> 00:02:02.229
Keep pull requests small,

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:04.109
let CI and Lenners take the first

00:02:04.109 --> 00:02:04.669
pass,

00:02:04.669 --> 00:02:06.349
and don't start what you can't review

00:02:06.349 --> 00:02:07.199
this week.

00:02:07.856 --> 00:02:08.456
Finally,

00:02:08.456 --> 00:02:09.746
agents read your history.

00:02:09.996 --> 00:02:11.976
Git log and git blame are documentation

00:02:11.976 --> 00:02:13.476
they consult directly.

00:02:13.476 --> 00:02:15.396
A history of fix bug leaves them

00:02:15.396 --> 00:02:16.136
guessing.

00:02:16.136 --> 00:02:17.336
Every commit message,

00:02:17.336 --> 00:02:19.916
branch name and pull request is context

00:02:19.916 --> 00:02:21.036
for the next agent,

00:02:21.036 --> 00:02:22.636
so make it worth reading.

00:02:22.636 --> 00:02:23.496
Next up:

00:02:23.496 --> 00:02:25.186
testing as the feedback loop.
